## Hunt results

### H1 — Stubs that look production after cap rename → **HIGH (greenwash class)**

**Pattern:** CAP-G6 WIP renames param types / comments to granular tokens while bodies remain residual stubs, dead `Ok` after `process_exit`, or policy-only evaluators. File/module names (`*_engine`, `*_ops`, `mtls_*`, `wireguard`) read as product.

#### H1a — Net rename facades (dirty WIP)

| Path | Cap after rename | Body truth |
|------|------------------|------------|
| `std/src/net.oo` | `http_get(http: &HttpCap)` → `fetch` | thin product-shaped facade (OK if check/runtime seal holds) |
| `std/src/net/wireguard.oo` | `&UdpCap` on interface + entries | **all effectful ops `process_exit(1)`** — not VPN |
| `std/src/ooda/sec/net/mtls_stubs.oo` | `mtls_perform_socket_handshake_raw(tcp: &TcpCap, …)` | `process_exit(1)` then dead `return Ok(dummy_session)` with `negotiated_cipher: "TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384"` |
| `mtls_verify_ocsp_stapling_response(tcp: &TcpCap, …)` | TcpCap | `process_exit(1)` |
| `mtls_parse_x509_der_asn1(sys: &SysCap, …)` | SysCap | `process_exit(1)` + dead dummy cert |

**Risk:** caller holding only the new preferred cap appears least-privilege-migrated; runtime path is still stub/exit. Cap migration **does not** create TLS/VPN.

#### H1b — Fs rename + unimplemented (dirty WIP)

| Path | Cap | Body |
|------|-----|------|
| `std/src/fs.oo` | FsRead/FsWrite | real sealed `fs_read`/`fs_write` (OK product-shaped) |
| `sec/cps/misra.oo` | `misra_run_linter(fs: &FsReadCap, …)` | residual read path |
| `misra_deviation_register(fs: &FsWriteCap, …)` | FsWriteCap | **`return Err("Unimplemented: … FsWriteCap file FFI…")`** |
| `secoc_persisted_freshness_{load,save}` | FsRead/FsWrite | **Err unimplemented** |

Rename is honest only when paired with Err/exit. Residual: type surface still looks API-complete.

#### H1c — M172 engine/ops “complete module” look

Across `src/ooda/{sec,crypto,aero,auto,bio,…}`: `*_engine.oo` / `*_ops.oo` / `*_types.oo` / shims present (M172). **55/61** `*_engine.oo` carry SysCap markers; many have no sealed process op. Example synthetic crypto (looks like production encrypt):

```text
// std/src/ooda/crypto/fhe_ops.oo — fhe_encrypt
c0_str = "c0_" + pub_pk0 + "_" + val_str
… is_valid: true; return Ok(cipher_res)
// fhe_decrypt recovers value ≈ checksum % plain_modulus — not FHE
```

BFV/CKKS rotate stubs rewrite polynomials as strings `"rot_" + step + "(" + c0 + ")"` and **`return Ok(res)`** without `process_exit` — production-shaped soft math.

#### H1d — `_with_capability(sys: &SysCap)` theater

≥22 `*_with_capability` entry points take `&SysCap` then **ignore** it and call soft core:

| Example | Behavior |
|---------|----------|
| `auth_engine_authenticate_with_capability` | `return auth_engine_authenticate(…)` — sys unused |
| `rbac_authorize_with_capability` | soft authorize → `Ok(true)` / Err |
| `fhe_{en,de}crypt_with_capability` | forwards to synthetic FHE |
| `zkp_verifier_verify_with_capability` | soft verify → `Ok(true)` |
| `plonk_verifier_verify_with_capability` | soft gate eval → `Ok(true)` |
| `*_engine_verify_with_capability` (dilithium, stark, bulletproof, …) | same wrap pattern |

**Severity:** HIGH honesty. Cap param invents a sealed-looking API without process seal or crypto seal.

